# Plan work — isolated git history (worktrees first)

Use **one dedicated branch per Plan**, and implement it in a **sibling git worktree** — never by switching the branch of the folder Cursor already has open. Commit-message tags alone are not enough for isolation.

## Why

`git checkout plan/<slug>` in a workspace root changes the branch shown in the IDE **Repositories** sidebar. Editing only under a separate worktree path leaves the IDE roots on `develop` / `master` / `main`.

## Branch naming

Derive a short slug from the plan file under `.cursor/plans/`:

- Prefer the plan filename stem before the hash, e.g. `pre-predicate_plot_background_6fd60bec.plan.md` → `pre-predicate-plot-background`
- Or kebab-case the plan frontmatter `name` field
- Branch: `plan/<slug>` (example: `plan/pre-predicate-plot-background`)

Use the **same slug** across every git repo you touch in this workspace (Spike3D, pyPhoPlaceCellAnalysis, etc.).

## When executing a Plan

1. **Start (before first code edit):** Prefer Cursor **`/worktree`** for this chat when the user started with it (IDE-native isolated checkout + later `/apply-worktree`). Otherwise, for each repo the plan will modify, from that repo’s **primary** root (the path already open in the IDE):
   - Create branch `plan/<slug>` from an up-to-date default branch **without checking it out in the primary tree**, e.g. `git branch plan/<slug> <default>` (or `git worktree add -b plan/<slug> …`).
   - Add a sibling worktree: `git worktree add ../wt-plan-<slug> plan/<slug>` (adjust the relative parent if needed so the path sits next to the repo, not inside it).
   - **All plan file edits and plan commits happen only under that worktree path.**
2. **Never** run `git checkout`, `git switch`, or otherwise change `HEAD` in an IDE-open workspace root for plan work. Say which worktree path you are editing.
3. **During:** Commit only changes that belong to this plan (in the worktree). Update the plan file (todo status) on the same branch when applicable.
4. **Unrelated work:** If the user asks for something outside the plan, do not edit the plan worktree for that — use the primary checkout or a different worktree/branch. Do not fold unrelated fixes into `plan/<slug>`.
5. **Commits (when the user asks):** Stage plan-scoped paths only. Use messages like `plan(<slug>): <what>` so `git log --grep=plan(<slug>)` filters cleanly.

## Parallel you + other agents

| Need | Approach |
|------|----------|
| One plan (default) | Sibling worktree `../wt-plan-<slug>` on `plan/<slug>`; IDE roots stay on default branch |
| User started with `/worktree` | Use that Cursor worktree; finish with `/apply-worktree` or merge when asked |
| Two plans at once | Two branches + **two worktrees**; never branch-switch the primary tree |
| Plan spans multiple repos | Same `plan/<slug>` name; one sibling worktree **per** repo |

## Finishing

- Merge or open a PR from `plan/<slug>` into the default branch when the user asks (merge from primary: `git merge plan/<slug>`, or `/apply-worktree` if using Cursor worktrees).
- Remove the sibling worktree when the user asks: `git worktree remove ../wt-plan-<slug>` (and delete the branch only when they say so).
- Do not delete the branch until merged unless the user says so.

## Avoid

- `git checkout` / `git switch` of `plan/<slug>` (or any other branch) in an IDE-open workspace root
- Implementing a plan entirely on `main` / `develop` / a long-lived feature branch shared with other tasks
- Committing unrelated files “while you’re here” on `plan/<slug>`
- Relying only on commit prefixes without a dedicated branch + worktree
